# 18. Trees and Forests

Active Directory can contain multiple domains.

## Tree

A **tree** is a collection of related domains that share a contiguous DNS namespace.

Example:

```text
example.com
   │
   ├── uk.example.com
   └── asia.example.com
```

These domains form an AD **tree**.

---

## Forest

A **forest** is a collection of one or more AD trees.

Different trees can have different DNS namespaces.

Example:

```text
Forest
│
├── Tree 1
│   ├── example.com
│   └── uk.example.com
│
└── Tree 2
    ├── example.net
    └── asia.example.net
```

### Easy Memory

```text
Domain
  ↓
Tree = multiple related domains
  ↓
Forest = one or more trees
```

> A forest can contain a single tree, so a forest does not necessarily mean "many companies."

---

# 19. Trust Relationships

When multiple domains or forests need to work together, **trust relationships** can allow users from one domain to access resources in another domain.

Example:

```text
THM UK Domain
      ↓
   Trust
      ↓
THM Asia Domain
```

A user from one domain may then be able to access resources in another domain, provided the appropriate permissions are also granted.

### Two-Way Trust

A two-way trust allows both domains to trust each other for authentication/authorisation relationships.

```text
Domain A  ⇄  Domain B
```

This means users from each side can potentially be authorised to access resources on the other side, depending on permissions.

